WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.740
Let's say, hypothetically,

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.810
that I made some bad financial bets.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.760
Maybe I was irresponsible
with a credit card.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.040
Maybe I got involved in
a certain shaped scheme.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.770
I've got a lot of debt
and I gotta get out of it.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.330
Well, companies go bankrupt all the time.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.560
Can I do that?

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.470
So I've got no way to pay off this debt.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.380
Creditors are hounding me,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.040
and the stress is just
building and building.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.660
I've gotta go find a lawyer,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.957
specifically a bankruptcy lawyer.

00:00:37.957 --> 00:00:39.700
My name is Stephen Starr.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.280
I'm an attorney here in New York.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.037
I practice bankruptcy and restructuring

00:00:44.037 --> 00:00:46.590
with a law firm, Starr and Starr PLC.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:50.410
When should a person
consider personal bankruptcy?

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:51.750
So that's a good question.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.950
As a bankruptcy attorney,

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.020
we're not trying to push
your sale bankruptcy.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.830
We're always looking at
what's the best solution

00:00:58.830 --> 00:00:59.779
for an individual.

00:00:59.779 --> 00:01:03.660
The first question is what are your debts?

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.490
Which is a total of how
much money do you owe

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:09.610
in terms of things like
credit cards or back rent

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.050
or student loans?

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:11.930
And then what's your income?

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.970
What's the money that's coming in?

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:14.980
And we compare those.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.870
And if you have more money
coming in than you owe,

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.520
and it's not too burdensome,

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.110
it's somewhere below, let's say, 50%,

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.670
which a lot of people in New
York pay really high rent.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:30.210
Maybe people can get out of
it through budgeting, right?

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.450
I'm a big fan of budgeting,

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.010
but if you're in a really deep hole,

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:34.590
budgeting is not gonna help.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:35.830
A lot of my clients,

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:39.420
it's literally their debt is
a multiple of their income.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.680
So they owe a hundred
thousand, they earn 50,

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.280
and that's gross numbers before taxes.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.180
The other thing is
people are about to start

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.540
the next chapter of their life.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:50.520
And what do I mean by that?

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:51.687
Someone was an entrepreneur.

00:01:51.687 --> 00:01:53.009
They had a business,

00:01:53.009 --> 00:01:54.870
it didn't work out,

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.130
and now they're gonna do something else.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:00.210
Well, should they keep
paying back all the debt

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.040
that they incurred in the failed business?

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.910
Or should they work on
the next chapter life?

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:06.961
So if a person is considering it,

00:02:06.961 --> 00:02:08.840
what should their first step be?

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.200
Is that to reach out to an attorney?

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.140
I don't view bankruptcy as a first resort.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.520
I do view it as a last resort.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.230
So I think the first step,

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.730
and you might be surprised to hear this,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.220
people need to get control

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:21.450
of just understanding the numbers.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.650
Sometimes clients come in

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:25.730
and they'll have all
these envelopes in a bag

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.290
and they're not opening their mail

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.370
'cause they're dealing from fear,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.361
literally making piles and figuring out

00:02:32.361 --> 00:02:33.640
how much do you owe?

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.480
And adding that up and
looking at your expenses.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.010
Could you explain to me the difference

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.650
between chapter seven and chapter 13?

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.440
Chapter 13 is really useful for people

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.457
who need the chance to catch
up with payments over time.

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:49.930
We would usually use that if someone has,

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:52.040
if they own a home and
they're facing a foreclosure

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.690
or they've fallen behind
on their payments.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.100
It's gonna be a really helpful tool,

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:59.100
I think, for people bouncing back

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.260
after all these COVID troubles,

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.620
than if people lost their jobs,

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.630
but then found a new job,

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:08.300
they can then catch up
over as much as five years

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:09.743
through a chapter 13 plan.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:16.990
It's like a repayment plan.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.148
It's a court-enforced repayment plan.

00:03:19.148 --> 00:03:20.820
In the chapter seven,

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.720
what assets do you get to keep?

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.650
And what gets taken away?

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.120
Seven is a liquidation,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.540
and the idea there is you
filing a bankruptcy case.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.900
Most people's debt, again,

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:35.210
going back to broad examples,

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:39.220
90, 98, 99% of my clients debts

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.710
do get wiped out, with the
exception of student loans

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:44.482
and certain tax debts.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.360
Most individuals, most of my clients,

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.233
when they file chapter seven,

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.930
they get to keep all their property.

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.000
But the way chapter seven works,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.520
there's a person called
the bankruptcy trustee.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.980
So an example I often use is
if someone had a collection

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.610
of luxury cars, Porsche,
Lamborghini, Maserati, Ferrari

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.220
very high-end, luxury automobiles,

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.730
the law is not gonna
let you keep automobiles

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:13.910
with that level of value.

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:16.540
It's gonna give you some money back,

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:19.040
but the trustee would hire an auctioneer,

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.620
have an auction, get bankruptcy
court permission to do that,

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.840
take that money, pay the creditor.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.144
For both chapter seven or 13,

00:04:26.144 --> 00:04:28.240
maybe the answer is different.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.200
What is the long-term impact
on your financial health?

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.800
There's something called the
Fair Credit Reporting Act,

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.020
which governs credit reporting.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.260
And that says that the credit grantors

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.770
can look to bankruptcy up to 10 years.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:45.110
But the received wisdom

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:50.110
is if you file a seven,

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:51.960
you're gonna rebuild your credit

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:53.950
in much faster time than that,

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.713
and at the end of the process,

00:04:55.713 --> 00:04:58.930
most of my clients are able
to rebuild their credit

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:00.070
within a few years.

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:02.960
There is life after bankruptcy, right?

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.772
They don't wanna leave people

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:09.510
penniless and homeless
as a result of bankruptcy

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.057
It's starting to sound
like personal bankruptcy

00:05:11.057 --> 00:05:12.540
might be for me,

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.530
but it would really help
if I could talk to somebody

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.490
who's been through this
process personally.

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:19.530
I've been through bankruptcy

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.470
and I can tell you all about it, Henry.

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:22.800
Oh, wow, really?

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:23.800
Who are you?

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.960
My name is Donald Ash.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:30.050
And yes, I'm a teacher
that had to file bankruptcy

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.670
probably about a decade ago.

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:32.503
Tell me your story.

00:05:32.503 --> 00:05:34.500
Why did you have to
file in the first place?

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.642
I got into a bit of a business scam

00:05:38.642 --> 00:05:41.200
that ended up stacking
up so much debt on me

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.400
that I couldn't pay it off.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:47.220
So because I was a teacher
not making much money

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.380
I kinda got into a business deal

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.310
that I didn't research very well,

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:51.400
friend recommended it,

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:55.382
and once everything was said and done

00:05:55.382 --> 00:05:56.960
I think I had racked up

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.410
almost $100,000 in debt.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.850
How did you decide to file?

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.320
How did you find out, "This
is the best option for me?"

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.450
I actually just kind of
started calling around

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:09.980
to see what lawyers can help me.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:12.200
And after getting turned
away from a couple,

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.890
one recommended me to a
lawyer and we gotta sit down

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:18.420
and go through just everything

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.200
that was going on with my finances

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.790
and figure out what I could
discharge and what I couldn't.

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:27.670
Beyond just the thing that
happened with the business scam,

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.226
it also gets rid of...

00:06:31.226 --> 00:06:32.900
So that's auto loans.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:35.959
I think I had a credit
card for like $1,000,

00:06:35.959 --> 00:06:37.093
a Discover Card.

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:42.700
And I might've had a
little bit of medical money

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:45.100
to pay back, but it wasn't a lot.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:46.510
But that also got discharged.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:49.490
So at first, I was just
thinking maybe I just should

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:52.897
just put the business stuff
on there and worry about that.

00:06:52.897 --> 00:06:56.540
But in the end, I put on everything

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:57.723
that I could put on there.

00:06:57.723 --> 00:06:59.110
And I just said, "It's fine.

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:00.800
If we're going to do this,

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:04.330
just get rid of all, just everything

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:05.520
that's not student loans.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:08.000
So I just have one thing to focus on."

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.540
How was this process for you,

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:12.250
I guess, mentally and emotionally

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:13.597
while it was going on?

00:07:13.597 --> 00:07:15.130
And even now, I suppose?

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:18.180
I have to say, it's probably
one of the hardest things

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:19.150
I've ever done.

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:21.110
It's like repo man, repossession people,

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:22.570
going to your mom's house

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:24.510
in the the wee hours of the morning.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:25.990
And she doesn't know what's going on.

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:29.797
And I'm like, "That's not
what this was supposed to be."

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:34.420
It was a big mess.

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.330
It was a really big financial mess.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.010
One thing I can say is, if you do file,

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:42.685
know that you can turn things around.

00:07:42.685 --> 00:07:45.530
It's not impossible, but
you have to keep in mind

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:47.630
that things have to change
for you, financially.

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:49.580
You have to make some changes

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.180
to make sure that change can happen.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:52.770
It is an option,

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:53.603
and I'm proof

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:57.710
that you can file bankruptcy
and get through it

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:59.450
and come out on the other side,

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:01.073
because yeah, my credit,

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:04.140
it's in the high 700s right now.

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:05.940
So it's back to a workable.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:08.500
Yeah, that's fantastic.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:10.652
At it's lowest point,
it was in the low 500s,

00:08:10.652 --> 00:08:12.905
even high 400s.

00:08:12.905 --> 00:08:16.090
It was a dark time, but
you can get through it

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:17.750
if you set a plan for yourself.

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:20.180
With the economic calamity
caused by the pandemic,

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:22.620
you'd expect bankruptcies
to be through the roof,

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.620
but that's actually not the case.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.530
I'm gonna talk to Jack Pitcher about it.

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:29.933
He is right over there in
the Bloomberg Building.

00:08:32.182 --> 00:08:34.160
Yeah, my name is Jack Pitcher.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:37.290
I am a reporter on the Personal
Finance and Wealth Teams.

00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:41.072
You're not seeing a lot
of bankruptcy declarations

00:08:41.072 --> 00:08:43.560
across the country right now.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:47.100
We've actually seen a decrease
in personal bankruptcies.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.370
What are the reasons
for that, do you think?

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:51.220
So it's pretty much coming down

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:53.660
to all of the measures
that have been enacted

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:55.433
to help people,

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:59.700
the stimulus payments, the
moratorium on evictions

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:03.540
and foreclosures, auto lenders
and credit card lenders

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:04.900
have been more lenient

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:06.480
with people who are late on payments

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:07.800
than they typically are.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:11.120
So that's preventing a lot of the events

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:13.963
that typically actually
push people into bankruptcy.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:17.060
Usually when someone, an individual,

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:18.170
is filing for bankruptcy,

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:21.130
it's because all of a sudden
their car is being repossessed

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:22.090
and they can't drive to work,

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:24.810
or their home is being foreclosed on.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:27.360
Those events that typically
actually push someone

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:30.000
to go talk to a lawyer and
ask if they need to file,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.115
there's been less of that
since the pandemic began.

00:09:32.115 --> 00:09:35.210
There are weirdly resilient careers

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:39.070
that thrive during times like this,

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:42.670
like repo men and bankruptcy
protection lawyers.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:45.549
So like those people aren't yet seeing

00:09:45.549 --> 00:09:47.440
a boom in business.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:48.273
Correct.

00:09:48.273 --> 00:09:50.740
Actually, I talked to a
number of bankruptcy lawyers

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:51.900
reporting on this story,

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:53.090
and a lot of them told me

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:55.793
they're having one of
their quieter years ever.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:59.760
Most of them are expecting a big pickup,

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:02.400
but for the most part
that hasn't started yet.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:04.230
Yeah, is there a sense on
when that might happen?

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.350
I've heard a lot of people
say a lot of different things.

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:09.350
Everyone seems to agree
that a wave is coming

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:12.370
with the level of unemployment
we saw in March and April.

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:15.270
Not very many people have
a great idea of when.

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:16.930
It will likely have to do

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:19.900
when some of these protections stop.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:22.508
And next year when we see the
foreclosure moratorium end

00:10:22.508 --> 00:10:25.910
and some of these other things,

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:28.470
There are a couple of things
that have already ended

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:30.600
that maybe it could lead to a pickup

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:32.060
in the fourth quarter of this year,

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:34.300
like the enhanced unemployment benefits.

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.410
It's been a long time since
there was a stimulus payment.

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:38.850
So there likely are gonna be more people

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:40.810
who are cash stressed the rest of the year

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:42.180
than there have been so far.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.560
Maybe we're looking at a ticking time bomb

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:45.640
waiting to go off.

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:47.760
If you're considering personal bankruptcy,

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:48.763
you're not alone.

